# Component Extraction Rules

## Purpose

These rules define when to extract Component Objects and when not to.

The goal is to avoid both extremes:

```text
Bad: everything inside one giant Page Object
Bad: tiny artificial components for every locator
Good: Page Object as route/screen owner
Good: Component Object as meaningful UI block owner
```

---

## When Component Object Is Justified

A Component Object is justified when at least one condition is true:

- the UI block is reused across multiple pages or suites;
- the UI block has multiple related locators and actions;
- the UI block has a stable root locator;
- the UI block represents a real reusable UI area;
- the UI block represents a meaningful semantic UI area;
- extracting it reduces meaningful duplication;
- extracting it prevents a Page Object from becoming too large;
- extracting it makes AI-generated changes more predictable and localized.

Examples of meaningful UI blocks:

- header;
- sidebar;
- modal;
- toast;
- table;
- filter panel;
- sorting control;
- pagination;
- result list;
- form section;
- cart summary.

---

## When Not To Create Component Objects

Do not create a Component Object when:

- the UI element is a single locator with no behavior;
- the UI element is used only once and adds no readability;
- the logic is trivial and does not represent a UI block;
- extraction increases complexity without clear benefit;
- the abstraction is created "just in case";
- the wrapper only renames Playwright methods.

Bad:

```ts
export class SubmitButtonComponent {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {}

  async click(): Promise<void> {
    await this.page.getByTestId("submit").click();
  }
}
```

Better:

```ts
await formPage.submitButton.click();
```

or:

```ts
await formPage.submit();
```

---

## Anti-God Page Object Rule

A Page Object must not become a dumping ground for all UI behavior.

Consider extracting Component Objects when one or more of these signs appears:

- the Page Object has too many public methods;
- the Page Object owns multiple unrelated UI areas;
- locator groups naturally belong together;
- sorting, filtering, listing, forms, tables, navigation, or modals are mixed together;
- the class becomes hard to read;
- AI-generated changes frequently touch unrelated methods;
- repeated locator/action patterns appear.

---

## Component Root Locator

Prefer Component Objects with a stable root locator.

Good:

```ts
export class HeaderComponent {
  constructor(private readonly root: Locator) {}

  readonly signInLink = this.root.getByTestId("nav-sign-in");
}
```

Acceptable when a stable root does not exist yet:

```ts
export class HeaderComponent {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {}

  readonly signInLink = this.page.getByTestId("nav-sign-in");
}
```

Do not invent fragile CSS just to create a component.

Bad:

```ts
this.root = page.locator("body > div:nth-child(2) > div > div:first-child");
```

---

## Component Ownership Policy

A Component Object should normally belong to a Page Object when the component is part of that page layout.

Good:

```ts
export class ListPage {
  readonly sorting: SortComponent;
  readonly results: ResultListComponent;

  constructor(page: Page) {
    this.sorting = new SortComponent(page);
    this.results = new ResultListComponent(page);
  }
}
```

Tests should normally access components through the Page Object.

Good:

```ts
await listPage.sorting.selectByValue("name-asc");

const names = await listPage.results.getNames();
```

Avoid:

```ts
test("sorts items", async ({ sorting, results }) => {
  await sorting.selectByValue("name-asc");

  const names = await results.getNames();
});
```

Use component fixtures only when direct access to the component is clearly needed across multiple suites.

---

## Component Fixture Rule

Do not provide Component Objects through fixtures by default.

Before creating a component fixture, verify:

- the component is reused in several suites;
- direct fixture access is clearer than Page Object composition;
- it does not create ownership confusion;
- it does not bypass the Page Object model;
- it does not create fixture explosion.

---

## Component Enforcement

If a UI block represents distinct behavior, consider extracting it into a Component Object when it introduces:

- reuse;
- real complexity;
- semantic UI boundary;
- Page Object growth;
- repeated locator/action patterns.

Do not automatically extract every visible UI block.

Component extraction must improve clarity, locality, or reuse.

---

## Main Principle

Extract components because they solve a real structure problem.

Do not extract components just because an element exists.

## Review Checklist

Before accepting Page Object or Component changes, verify:

- each Page Object represents one page, screen, route, or navigation boundary;
- each Component Object represents a meaningful UI block;
- components are accessed through Page Objects by default;
- no component fixtures were added without clear need;
- no scenario-specific assertions exist in Page Objects or Components;
- no `test.step` exists in Page Objects or Components;
- no test data generation exists in Page Objects or Components;
- no direct `process.env` access exists in Page Objects or Components;
- no cross-page business flow is hidden in Page Objects or Components;
- no speculative methods were added;
- no fragile locators were introduced;
- no God Page Object was created.